concepts.body:
Every standard container gets its memory through an **allocator**, the last template parameter: `std::vector<T, Alloc>`. The default, `std::allocator<T>`, calls `operator new`. A custom allocator only needs a `value_type`, `allocate(n)`, `deallocate(p, n)`, equality comparison and a converting constructor; `std::allocator_traits` fills in the rest:

```cpp
template <class T> struct Arena {
    using value_type = T;
    T* allocate(std::size_t n);
    void deallocate(T* p, std::size_t n) noexcept;
};
```

The classic model has drawbacks. The allocator is part of the type, so `vector<int, A>` and `vector<int, B>` don't mix, and stateful allocators bring tricky propagation rules on copy, move and swap.

C++17's **polymorphic memory resources** (`<memory_resource>`, namespace `std::pmr`) solve this. A `std::pmr::memory_resource` is an abstract class with virtual `allocate`/`deallocate`. `std::pmr::polymorphic_allocator` forwards to one, and aliases like `std::pmr::vector<T>` and `std::pmr::string` use it, so containers backed by different resources share one type. The allocator also propagates to elements: a `pmr::vector<pmr::string>` places its strings' buffers in the same resource.

Standard resources:

- `monotonic_buffer_resource`: bump-pointer arena. Allocation is a pointer increment, `deallocate` is a no-op, and everything is freed at once when the resource dies. Ideal for per-request or per-frame data, optionally starting from a stack buffer.
- `unsynchronized_pool_resource` / `synchronized_pool_resource`: pools of fixed-size blocks, without or with locking.
- `new_delete_resource()` and `null_memory_resource()` (which always throws, handy as an upstream to catch overflow).

The usual caveat: the resource must outlive every container using it, and measure before and after to confirm allocation was actually the bottleneck.

concepts.code:
#include <array>
#include <cstddef>
#include <iostream>
#include <memory_resource>
#include <string>
#include <vector>

int main() {
    std::array<std::byte, 4096> buffer;                        // arena on the stack
    std::pmr::monotonic_buffer_resource arena{
        buffer.data(), buffer.size(), std::pmr::null_memory_resource()};  // throw if exceeded

    std::pmr::vector<std::pmr::string> names{&arena};          // vector AND strings use the arena
    names.reserve(8);
    names.emplace_back("a string long enough to need its own heap buffer");
    names.emplace_back("short");

    bool same = names[0].get_allocator().resource() == &arena; // allocator propagated
    std::cout << names.size() << ' ' << same << '\n';          // 2 1

    std::pmr::unsynchronized_pool_resource pool;               // size-class pools
    std::pmr::vector<int> nums{&pool};
    nums.assign(1000, 7);
    std::cout << nums.back() << '\n';                          // 7
}   // arena memory is released all at once; monotonic deallocate() is a no-op

#include <coroutine>
#include <iostream>
#include <utility>

template <typename T>
class Generator {
public:
    struct promise_type {
        T current{};
        Generator get_return_object() {
            return Generator{std::coroutine_handle<promise_type>::from_promise(*this)};
        }
        std::suspend_always initial_suspend() noexcept { return {}; }   // lazy start
        std::suspend_always final_suspend() noexcept { return {}; }     // keep frame for done()
        std::suspend_always yield_value(T v) noexcept { current = v; return {}; }
        void return_void() noexcept {}
        void unhandled_exception() { throw; }
    };
    explicit Generator(std::coroutine_handle<promise_type> h) : h_{h} {}
    Generator(Generator&& o) noexcept : h_{std::exchange(o.h_, {})} {}
    ~Generator() { if (h_) h_.destroy(); }                              // frees the frame
    bool next() { h_.resume(); return !h_.done(); }
    T value() const { return h_.promise().current; }
private:
    std::coroutine_handle<promise_type> h_;
};

Generator<int> fib(int n) {                     // co_yield makes this a coroutine
    int a = 0, b = 1;
    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) { co_yield a; b = a + b; a = b - a; }
}

int main() {
    auto g = fib(8);
    while (g.next()) std::cout << g.value() << ' ';   // 0 1 1 2 3 5 8 13
    std::cout << '\n';   // C++23: std::generator<int> from <generator> replaces all of this
}

#include <chrono>
#include <iostream>
#include <list>
#include <numeric>
#include <vector>

template <typename F>
long long time_us(F f) {
    auto t0 = std::chrono::steady_clock::now();
    f();
    auto dt = std::chrono::steady_clock::now() - t0;
    return std::chrono::duration_cast<std::chrono::microseconds>(dt).count();
}

int main() {
    const int n = 5'000'000;
    std::vector<int> vec;
    vec.reserve(n);                         // one allocation instead of ~30
    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) vec.push_back(i % 7);
    std::list<int> lst(vec.begin(), vec.end());   // same data, one node per element

    long long sum = 0;
    auto tv = time_us([&] { sum += std::accumulate(vec.begin(), vec.end(), 0LL); });
    auto tl = time_us([&] { sum += std::accumulate(lst.begin(), lst.end(), 0LL); });
    std::cout << "vector " << tv << " us, list " << tl << " us, sum " << sum << '\n';
    // build with -O2: the list is typically several times slower (pointer chasing)
}
